# OpenSSL release-signing public key — the provenance root for the FIPS provider (tap#221).
#
# OpenSSL <openssl@openssl.org>  (the project release key, created 2026-05-26)
# Primary fingerprint: B146 647E 45A7 B339 47AB 226B 2A2C 87D1 6169 2D40
#
# ROTATED 2026-09-02 with the move to OpenSSL 3.0.22 (decision D17, tap#295). The previous
# anchor was Tomáš Mráz's personal key A21F AB74 B008 8AA3 6115 2586 B8EF 1A6B A9DA 2D5C,
# which signed 3.0.9; 3.0.22 is signed by a SUBKEY of this project key instead, and the
# build's signature gate refused the old anchor — exactly as designed. Both primaries are
# listed in doc/fingerprints.txt at the openssl-3.0.22 tag; this one is the signer.
#
# WHAT THIS IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT
# This file is the KEY. It is not the signature. openssl-<version>.tar.gz.asc — the detached
# signature — is fetched fresh from the release URL on every build and is never committed;
# docker/build-openssl-fips.sh downloads it beside the tarball and prints the URL it used.
# Committing the key and fetching the signature is the deliberate split: the signature must
# be current with the artifact, while the key is the trust anchor and so must be reviewed by
# a human exactly once, in a diff, rather than trusted fresh on every build.
#
# WHERE IT CAME FROM
#   Retrieved 2026-09-02 from the project's AUTHORITATIVE key bundle — the one the source
#   page names — not from a keyserver (keys.openpgp.org serves this key without its user ID
#   and strips third-party signatures; keyserver copies carry no cross-certification):
#
#     curl -sSL https://openssl-library.org/source/pubkeys.asc -o pubkeys.asc
#       (sha256 56e106cd1c44bdb1… on 2026-09-02)
#     gpg --import pubkeys.asc
#     gpg --export --armor B146647E45A7B33947AB226B2A2C87D161692D40 \
#         > docker/openssl-release-keys.asc      # NOT export-minimal: keep the cross-cert
#
# CROSS-CERTIFICATION (the chain from the previous release key), verbatim from
#   gpg --check-sigs B146647E45A7B33947AB226B2A2C87D161692D40 against that bundle:
#
#     uid           OpenSSL <openssl@openssl.org>
#     sig!3    N   2A2C87D161692D40 2026-05-26  [self-signature]
#     sig!         216094DFD0CB81EF 2026-06-01  OpenSSL <openssl@openssl.org>
#
#   216094DFD0CB81EF is the previous project release key BA54 73A2 B058 7B07 FB27 CF2D 2160
#   94DF D0CB 81EF (created 2024-04-08), which certified this primary on 2026-06-01, before
#   its first use (2026-08-25, all five release lines at once). The rotation brief that
#   authorized this anchor is in the bump PR body (bump-openssl-fips skill §3a).
#
# WHY THIS KEY IS AUTHORIZED
#   doc/fingerprints.txt AT THE openssl-3.0.22 TAG lists this primary as authorized to sign
#   OpenSSL releases:
#     https://raw.githubusercontent.com/openssl/openssl/openssl-3.0.22/doc/fingerprints.txt
#   The tag's own copy is the authority, not the current list on openssl-library.org: that
#   one names only CURRENTLY-ACTIVE signers and will not vouch for an older release.
#   openssl-3.0.22.tar.gz.asc is signed by SUBKEY 64ED7B1DCCE71CB2 (fingerprint C46E D3F2
#   CBEF DA1F DAAD A442 64ED 7B1D CCE7 1CB2) under this primary. The .asc names the subkey
#   while fingerprints.txt lists primaries; comparing the two directly makes an authorized
#   key look unlisted. The build asserts the PRIMARY.
#
# HOW TO RE-VERIFY THIS FILE (no trust in this header required)
#     gpg --show-keys --with-fingerprint docker/openssl-release-keys.asc
#   Compare the printed primary fingerprint to the one above and to the tag's
#   fingerprints.txt. Do NOT compare file digests between exports: armored output is not
#   byte-stable across gpg versions or fetch dates, so the FINGERPRINT is the thing that is
#   comparable and the file digest is not.
#
# ROTATION / DRIFT
#   Nothing here expires on a schedule, and nothing automated bumps it. A newer key is
#   needed only when a release is signed by a different OpenSSL team member — a bump this
#   file shares with the pins in docker/build-openssl-fips.sh. Whether upstream still vouches
#   for this fingerprint is a live question, not a settled one: tap#231 tracks re-asserting it
#   against upstream on a schedule rather than assuming a reviewed-once file stays true.
